WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:26.840
Navier-Stokes equations describe how fluids move. In September twenty twenty six, OpenAI published a solution of the three-dimensional existence and smoothness problem, with a Lean formalization. Smooth forcing can drive a fluid initially at rest to a finite-time singularity: its speed grows without bound while energy stays finite. Let's explain that result, then explore real Osprey code and its proof workbench.

00:27.690 --> 00:46.495
Open the Navier-Stokes lesson in Osprey. Velocity tells us how the fluid moves. Pressure pushes it around. Viscosity spreads out motion and removes energy from this flow. The lesson writes these relationships as equations, with a second equation requiring the fluid to stay incompressible.

00:49.823 --> 01:07.803
This is the actual Osprey program used for the picture. It calculates the flow’s spatial shape and how its strength falls over time. The renderer uses those numbers to draw the moving fluid. Separate numerical checks measure how closely the calculated field satisfies the equations.

01:08.653 --> 01:29.091
Watch the four rotating cells lose strength. No external force keeps them going. The energy readout falls as viscosity dissipates the motion. This is a known exact solution in two dimensions, with a domain that repeats at its edges. Osprey evaluates its formula, so we can compare the calculation with a known answer.

01:29.941 --> 01:55.367
The published proof has a precise statement and a Lean formalization; the lesson also supplies a much smaller propositional exercise. Assume there are two possibilities, and one has been ruled out. Show that the other must hold. Write the assumptions, consider each case, and watch Osprey check the proof. The words smooth and blowup name propositions here; the exercise teaches how a conclusion follows from assumptions.

01:56.217 --> 02:15.767
Open the lesson to explore the symbols and try the remaining-outcome exercise. Osprey already checks selected supporting lemmas from the published OpenAI sources. The complete Navier-Stokes proof is a larger port still to do. You can run this fluid calculation and build your own small checked arguments today.
